# pulse starter: order-pipeline ({{.Lang}})

The golden workflow: a CRUD API with background jobs and persistence,
entirely offline.

```
POST /orders ──▶ api ──▶ DynamoDB put (status: pending)
                  └────▶ SQS send ──▶ worker ─▶ DynamoDB update (status: processed)
GET /orders/{id} ─▶ api ─▶ DynamoDB get — the real, current record
```

Dependencies were installed automatically by `pulse init`{{if eq .Lang "python"}} (a project
`.venv` with boto3 — pulse finds it on its own, no activation needed){{end}}. If that
was skipped or failed:

{{if eq .Lang "python"}}```bash
python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt
```{{else}}```bash
npm install
```{{end}}

The whole loop:

```bash
pulse start
curl -X POST localhost:3000/orders -d '{"sku":"A1","qty":2}'   # → 201, status "pending"
# a second later the worker marks it processed…
curl localhost:3000/orders/<id>                                 # → status "processed" ✓
```

More to try: `pulse logs worker -f` (live jobs) · `pulse send order-events
'{"id":"x"}'` (push a job by hand) · the DLQ demo `curl -X POST
localhost:3000/orders -d '{"sku":"X","fail":true}'` (3 retries → dead-letter,
watch `pulse list`) · stop and restart pulse — orders and queued jobs survive.

pulse points the vanilla AWS SDK at its local mocks via `AWS_ENDPOINT_URL`,
so this code runs unmodified in real AWS.

Prefer the other language? `pulse init <name> --template order-pipeline --lang node|python`
